Trace:

  1. latestRoundData() → _checkSequencer() → _calculateBaseToQuote(). The sequencer gate is present only here.
  2. latestAnswer(), latestTimestamp(), latestRound() → _calculateBaseToQuote() directly. So yes, textually the V2 leg
  does skip _checkSequencer(). That part of the claim is factually accurate about the file.
  3. What does _calculateBaseToQuote() resolve to in the derived contract? PendlePTOracle._calculateBaseToQuote() reads
  baseToUSDOracle.latestRoundData(), requires baseToUSD > 0, optionally inverts, multiplies by _getPTRate() (Pendle TWAP
  over twapDuration). It returns the same tuple regardless of which entry point called it. So the V2 leg does not
  return a different or manipulable price than the V3 leg — it returns the identical derived value. The only difference
  is that during an L2 sequencer outage / grace window the V3 leg reverts and the V2 leg returns a (possibly stale)
  number.
  4. For that difference to be a Medium, some in-scope consumer must actually read the V2 leg and act on it. The code
  under review says the opposite: the contract's own docstring states it is "Used inside the TradingModule to calculate
  the price of arbitrary token pairs," and the V2-family helpers that are unambiguously unused are explicitly annotated
  "Unused in the trading module" and made to revert(). Nothing in either file shown — base or derived — contains a
  caller of latestAnswer/latestTimestamp/latestRound. The claim's asserted victim ("any integrator or on-chain
  health/liquidation path that reads the AggregatorV2 leg") is a hypothetical third party, not a traced in-scope call
  site. No shown protocol path reaches these functions.
  5. Absent a named in-scope consumer, the unprivileged attacker gains nothing: calling latestAnswer() yourself is a
  view call that changes no state, moves no funds, and mints no debt. The "exploit" is entirely contingent on unshown
  external integrator code, i.e. speculative harm.
  6. The secondary startedAt == 0 sub-claim is about the gate that is invoked (latestRoundData). With startedAt == 0,
  block.timestamp - 0 is enormous and exceeds the grace period, so it reads healthy — but the attacker does not control
  the sequencer uptime feed's round data, so this is not an unprivileged trigger; it is a robustness nit on a trusted
  Chainlink feed, and it is orthogonal to the headline "V2 leg bypasses the check" claim (it would make the V3 leg
  permissive too, which weakens rather than supports a V2-vs-V3 divergence story).
  7. Also note the sequencer gate is a liveness/staleness guard, not an anti-manipulation guard: bypassing it does not
  let the attacker set a price, only read one that the underlying Chainlink aggregator and the Pendle TWAP already
  produced.

  I cannot point to a step in the shown code where an unprivileged actor causes an invariant break by reading the V2
  leg. Under the tie-break, this fails.

  CONSTRAINT|C15|A guard present on one read entry point but absent on a sibling read entry point is only a finding when
  the candidate names an in-scope consumer that actually reads the unguarded entry point and the concrete
  state-changing divergence that follows; a view function with no traced caller yields speculative harm contingent on
  unshown third-party code.
  VERDICT|REFUTED|src/oracles/AbstractCustomOracle.sol:latestAnswer:64|C15|Both entry points resolve to the identical
  derived _calculateBaseToQuote() value and no in-scope caller of the V2 leg exists in either the base or derived
  contract — the contract is documented as consumed via latestRoundData() by the TradingModule, so the "bypass" is a
  stateless view call whose only asserted victim is a hypothetical external integrator, and the startedAt == 0 sub-claim
